WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:14.170
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Welcome to the meeting of
the NHS England Board in public.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:23.330
The observent of you will have noticed that I am not Sir Malcolm Grant, he is ill.  Malcolm we wish you the very best.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:30.600
I'm assured we will have at least one person watching us on the web and I will undoubtedly get a performance review afterwards.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.500
Anyway, welcome to out meeting.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:47.580
I have an apology from Margaret Casely-Hayford, who is unfortunately unable to join us due to a confilct in scheduling.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:01:09.000
There is as always no fire alarm due this afternoon.  If one does sound, the board support team will escort all of us to a safe place and count us to ensure we are all here and that we have made it.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:15.380
Welcome to the meeting of the NHS England board in public.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:25.920
We have a full and very important agenda. Alongside
our normal reporting, we have both the Mental

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:32.759
Health Taskforce findings and the results
of the consultation following the Cancer Drugs

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:37.979
Fund work that has been ongoing for some period
of time.

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:46.320
Before I move into the formal agenda, can
I just ask if any members of our Board wish

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.789
to declare any agenda items where a conflict
of interest may occur, or to raise any new

00:01:51.789 --> 00:01:55.240
declaration of interest which has not already
been recorded? John.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:00.890
&gt;&gt;John Burn: In my professional academic capacity,
I am currently acting as an adviser to AstraZeneca

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.909
on one of their new cancer drugs, which does
not directly impact but is obviously relevant

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:05.770
for the Cancer Drug Fund issue.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.649
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: All right. Thank you, John.

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:13.270
&gt;&gt;Speaker: I am a company member of Cancer
Research UK.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.530
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Right, okay. Thank you.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:25.460
Can we now move to the minutes of the previous
meeting? Do any Board members have any comments

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:31.340
on the minutes? May I sign them? Thank you.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:40.920
There are no matters arising from those minutes
that are outstanding. Chief Executive's report,

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:42.500
which I understand is an oral report.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.970
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: It is, yes. Thanks, David.
So, it is less than a month since we last

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:53.090
met. We have got most of the items covered
elsewhere on the agenda, so I just want to

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:57.880
really place on record thanks to several people
for important work that has been done since

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:58.950
we last met.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:05.030
First of all, as we will be discussing later
on, the final report of the Mental Health

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:12.810
Taskforce, chaired by Paul Farmer and Jacqui
Dyer, launched with a great interest on the

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.150
part of the whole of the National Health Service,
but also local government, patients' groups

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:20.590
and others. We have got an item specifically
on that, so I will say nothing other than

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:24.480
to place on record our thanks to the taskforce.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:32.570
Similarly, this week we have published the
independent maternity review that we commissioned.

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:39.450
That group has again done an outstanding job
of bringing together a diverse range of stakeholders,

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:46.620
including parents, campaigning groups, patient
safety advocates, the Royal College of Obstetricians

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.040
and the Royal College of Midwives, and has
come up with some tangible propositions as

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:57.260
to how to improve the safety and the responsiveness
of maternity services for the 660,000 mums

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.880
who give birth in England each year.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:09.240
In March, in the context of our business plan
for next year, Jane will set out the actions

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.430
we will be taking to get going on implementation
there, but I just want to draw formally the

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.120
Board's attention to the fact that that has
been published.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:24.449
Thirdly, to give our thanks for the final
review that Stephen Bubb has done of the work

00:04:24.449 --> 00:04:29.819
on the learning disabilities implementation.
We have had a number of conversations here

00:04:29.819 --> 00:04:31.840
about that, under Jane's leadership.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:38.650
We have a clear game plan, including the closure
of Calderstones Hospital, and a 30-50% reduction

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:45.879
in outmoded inpatient facilities across the
country, but thanks to Stephen and his team

00:04:45.879 --> 00:04:49.539
for the work that they have done.

00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:56.090
She is not here with us today, but I also
want to thank Ros Roughton and team for the

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:03.939
constructive and successful way in which the
2016-17 negotiation on the GP contract was

00:05:03.939 --> 00:05:06.020
concluded with the BMA.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:13.509
As I said at the time, I think it is testament
to what constructive and flexible negotiation

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:20.039
can bring. However, it is obviously not the
total answer by any means as to what needs

00:05:20.039 --> 00:05:24.830
to change and improve in general practice,
and so we will be bringing forward a comprehensive

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:33.120
package of support for GPs shortly, in partnership
with the Royal College of GPs and the BMA

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.509
GPs committee.

00:05:34.509 --> 00:05:41.930
In the context of employee-related matters,
I also, finally, want to draw attention to

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:48.009
the fact that, this week, on behalf of the
National Health Service as a whole, we published

00:05:48.009 --> 00:05:54.139
the results of the NHS staff survey for 2015.

00:05:54.139 --> 00:06:00.099
299,000 members of staff from across the National
Health Service gave their views on what it

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:06.199
is like to work at their local hospital, ambulance
trust, mental health provider, community service

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:14.620
and other places as well. There was some positive
news there, as well as some ongoing sources

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:23.080
of concern. 80% of the 299,000 people told
us that they do feel able to do their job

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:26.580
to a standard that they are personally pleased
with, which is up from last year.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.840
Staff engagement across the Health Service
has actually been steadily rising over the

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:37.159
last four years, and is the highest it has
been in five years. 73% of staff said that

00:06:37.159 --> 00:06:42.159
their organisation's top priority was indeed
patient care; again, a further increase from

00:06:42.159 --> 00:06:48.990
last year. So those are welcome developments
which, perhaps, were not widely reported.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:53.029
Set against that, there are still far too
many staff across the National Health Service

00:06:53.029 --> 00:07:00.120
who report feeling unsupported at work, or
on the receiving end of discrimination, bullying

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.409
or harassment.

00:07:02.409 --> 00:07:07.279
The reality is that there are enormous differences
between different employers across the National

00:07:07.279 --> 00:07:12.330
Health Service, on those and every other measure
that we track in the staff survey. Indeed,

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:15.840
within NHS England we have seen that ourselves.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.689
The work on the workforce race equality standard
and the work that we are going to be kicking

00:07:19.689 --> 00:07:25.059
off next year on incentives for trusts around
employee health - we are going to be launching

00:07:25.059 --> 00:07:31.340
a new incentive scheme to that effect next
week - will all, I think, help move the dial

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:32.779
on some of those.

00:07:32.779 --> 00:07:37.469
The rest I think we will be covering in the
other items of the Board papers. Thanks.

00:07:37.469 --> 00:07:42.039
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Thanks, Simon. Any questions
for Simon?

00:07:42.039 --> 00:07:51.580
Simon, I have a couple. On the staff survey,
and in particular the NHS England element

00:07:51.580 --> 00:08:00.259
of the staff satisfaction survey, perhaps
you could just let us know how you are handling

00:08:00.259 --> 00:08:07.430
it. What are the major initiatives that you
are putting in place as part of that?

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:13.460
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Sure. I might turn to Karen
in a moment as well, but there are a set of

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:20.400
national things where we continue to raise
our game, and there are some big differences

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.439
between different teams across the country
as well in what those survey results look

00:08:24.439 --> 00:08:25.279
like.

00:08:25.279 --> 00:08:31.129
So we have a set of things to do for the organisation
as a whole. We have seen some recent improvements

00:08:31.129 --> 00:08:38.479
in scores after the impact of the redundancies
and the downsizing programme that we had last

00:08:38.479 --> 00:08:42.950
year, but we still see some pretty big differences
between teams around the country as well.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.440
Karen is leading that for us.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:54.150
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: There are definitely some
things that staff are concerned about in terms

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:59.040
of corporate services. We have a number of
things that we are hoping to start to really

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:05.330
make a difference to staff in the very near
future - the next few weeks - which are improvements

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:10.870
in recruitment processes and timescales, and
improvement in procurement processes and timescales.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:18.340
These are things which are just getting in
the way of staff working effectively. We have

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.250
made a lot of progress on those.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:30.530
On the more substantive issues about how well
staff feel supported and engaged by their

00:09:30.530 --> 00:09:38.600
managers and their organisations, we have
been doing quite a lot in terms of trying

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:41.870
to have the right conversations with staff.

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:52.920
For example, we had a very good session at
our leadership forum with our top 120 managers

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.530
to talk about the impacts of bullying and
what we might do about that, which I think

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:57.730
made a big difference.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:06.150
I think that is really an important part.
We are having conversations with directors,

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:13.780
mainly because directors are obviously the
people who really need to care about their

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:18.950
staff, and work on how they support and engage
their staff.

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:27.090
This is about us investing management time
in staff and supporting them, understanding

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:35.020
their concerns and then dealing with them.
I think that that is probably the most important

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:39.200
thing that we can do going forward, and that
is what we are, I think, making some progress

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:39.590
on.

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:43.660
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Moira, on our behalf you
are providing some input and oversight on

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:44.220
this particular vital subject.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:44.640
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Absolutely.

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:52.150
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Good. Thank you. I think
it is self-evident that when the pressure

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:59.110
is highest and the demands are at their most
intense, actually one of the things that can

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:06.330
often get left behind is the care, welfare,
engagement and communication with our colleagues,

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:10.990
and yet that is the thing that can actually
help us deliver when the times are at their

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:17.600
most intense. I would really encourage you,
with the directors and their immediate teams,

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:23.610
to recognise that that is not a cost; that
is an investment which will help in delivery.

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:27.090
We will come back to this subject, no doubt.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:33.510
Obviously, Simon, the GP programme is at the
heart of a lot of the changes. It is where,

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:40.300
again, the pressure can be most intense. When
is it likely that we will have a conversation

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:44.940
around the package that we can put in place?

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:53.950
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Without prejudging exactly
the timings on this, I would hope that we

00:11:53.950 --> 00:12:02.140
will have a package that has the support of
our key partners within the next six to eight

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:08.970
weeks. So we will take the opportunity to
discuss elements of that before that, obviously.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:12.550
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Therefore, in three or four
months, we will bring it back here for a discussion,

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:13.380
no doubt.

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:18.810
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes. I would certainly like
this published in so-called spring.

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:22.459
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Lovely. Okay, thank you.

